@@ -214,3 +214,33 @@ app.use(async (event) => {
 
 > [!NOTE]
 > Give each session a distinct `name`. Two sessions that share a name share the same cookie, so the last write wins.
+
+## Rotate the Password
+
+Changing `password` would normally sign out every user whose session was sealed with the old one. To avoid that, keep the previous passwords in `extraUnsealPasswords`. They are only used to open sessions, in order, after `password` fails. Sealing always uses `password`, so a session is moved to the new password the next time it is written or resealed.
+
+```js
+import { useSession } from "h3";
+
+app.use(async (event) => {
+  const session = await useSession(event, {
+    password: process.env.SESSION_PASSWORD_NEW,
+    extraUnsealPasswords: [process.env.SESSION_PASSWORD_OLD],
+  });
+
+  return session.data;
+});
+```
+
+To rotate safely:
+
+1. Deploy with the old password as `password` and the new one in `extraUnsealPasswords`. Every instance can then open sessions sealed with either one.
+2. Once every instance runs that config, swap them: the new password as `password`, the old one in `extraUnsealPasswords`.
+3. Remove the old password once the longest `maxAge` (or `idleTimeout`) of the sessions it sealed has passed.
+
+Keep these limits in mind:
+
+- Sessions that are never written and never expire stay sealed with the old password until they expire. Removing it earlier signs them out.
+- Anyone who has an old password can forge valid sessions for as long as it stays in the config. Rotating does not revoke existing sessions by itself.
+- Each extra password adds one PBKDF2 derivation for every request that carries an invalid session cookie.
+- Extra passwords need the same entropy as `password`, and the same minimum length.
